WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.680
Good morning, John!

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.760
About a week ago, I got on a train with Charlie McDonnell.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
We went through Swindon Town!

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.800
It was raining. And the doors of the train scared me because you didn't have to touch them.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.280
When I asked Charlie if he wanted to hang out while we were in the UK,

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.720
he said he wanted to take us to Bath,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:22.140
a city of 80,000 people that was declared by UNESCO in its entirety to be a World Heritage Site, so yeah.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.100
I guess it's a World Heritage Build-A-Bear there, and a World Heritage Pizza hut.

00:00:26.108 --> 00:00:27.480
It is a World Heritage abbey.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.670
It's a pretty nice abbey. I asked Charlie about the Bath Abbey.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.500
Charlie: It's painted in an episode of Community.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.000
Indeed.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.410
We had lunch with Charlie's mum,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.900
we talked about inequity and happiness,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.080
and she drove us on the wrong side of the road up a hill so that we can get a nice view of Bath.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.154
Honestly, I should be saying bath (regional English pronunciation)

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.240
because every time I said bath (American pronunciation),

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.720
people had no idea what I was talking about.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.580
The view was indeed nice though.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.280
Then, Charlie walked us down possibly the slipperiest path of all time.

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:50.680
Very dangerous.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:51.820
Then, down to the river, which was gorgeous.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.400
Then, up a hill past a bunch of fancy nineteenth-century houses and to the Royal Crescent.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.040
Then, back down to the baths, which is what Bath was named for.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.060
The Roman baths, constructed nearly 2,000 years ago, and still able to hold water.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.100
They are made of lead though, so don't drink it.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.440
Bath's hot spring made it one of the principal resources of the Roman occupation of Britain.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.420
The Romans built what was probably the largest structure in England around the spring,

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.420
converting it into kind of tourist attraction for wealthy Romans

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.320
that was in continuous use for hundreds of years,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.340
so long that their wooden shoes wore through the stone floors.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.091
Wealthy Romans would come for relaxation, for prayer,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.560
and to show off how rich they were, who could afford more attendants,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.160
more luxurious scented oils, that kind of thing.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.200
The more things change, the more they stay the same, right?

00:01:37.205 --> 00:01:38.339
But the baths were used by all kinds,

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:43.409
people would inscribe curses on pieces of pewter and then throw them into the baths with notes like,

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:45.000
"I think Jeff stole my gloves,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.540
could you please burn his eyes out of his head please?"

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.776
It's kinda beautiful to see how petty and silly we were then,

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.040
in the context of how petty and silly we continue to be.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.060
There's a wonder to that, and to our continued existence despite it.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.435
The Roman Baths, and the abbey and the Royal Crescent, and even the Marks &amp; Spencer,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.160
all those things, in many ways, are monuments to wealth.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.520
Sure they take different forms: housing, religion, relaxation, shopping,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.740
but they're all a display of the very excesses that we rightfully complain about today:

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.220
income inequality, concentration of wealth, frivolous spending.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.600
The Roman baths, after all, were built by the enslaved survivors of a war of conquest,

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.019
but in a way, that's why we enjoy them today.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.940
They are exceptional now because they were exceptional then,

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.640
and they were exceptional then because only a concentration of wealth and power made them possible.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.340
Most of the things that we tend to observe from our cultural history are artifacts of concentrated wealth,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.280
and the greatest and most enjoyed objects of our age will likely be as well,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.780
whether we're talking about the Burj Khalifa,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.760
or the iPhone, or the Harry Potter movies, or the Hubble Space Telescope.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.480
All of those things were extremely expensive,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.220
and all of them required a humongous concentration of the world's wealth

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:54.200
mostly,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.489
it has to be said, here in America.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:00.029
We talk a lot about the crazy income inequality within America,

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.783
but globally that's a much more upsetting picture.

00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:08.240
To be in the richest 1% of the world, you need to make a whopping $35000 per year.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.940
If you have $2000 in the bank,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.100
you're richer

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:12.980
than half the world .

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.200
We have to take notice of the amazing creations of our own age and consider

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.980
what the greatest cultural legacy would be,

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.640
the answer of course,

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.340
being peace, and plenty, and equality, and awesome.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.180
Charlie, thanks for taking me around Bath.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.600
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

